docs/design/dao.space.query.md:\\n   575\\t;;   - No schema: every attr is potentially multi-valued. Forward attrs\\n   576\\t;;     follow the entity-attrs convention (one datom -> scalar, more ->\\n   577\\t;;     vector). Reverse attrs (`:_attr`) always return a vector.\\n   578\\t;;   - Ref-ness is asserted by the pattern, not guessed. A nested map spec\\n   579\\t;;     navigates values as entity ids.\\n   580\\t;;   - Missing attrs are omitted, not nil-valued, unless the pattern gives\\n   581\\t;;     :default. `:db/id` is included in every pull result map.\\n   582\\t;;   - Recursion markers (`'...` / depth limits) are deferred: finite\\n   583\\t;;     patterns bound the walk by construction.\\n   584\\t;;   - Nested map specs ({:friend [...]}) do not support :limit/:as\\n   585\\t;;     (only flat vector elements do).\\n   586\\t\\n   587\\t(defn- wildcard-symbol?\\n   588\\t  [x]\\n   589\\t  (and (symbol? x) (= x '*)))\\n   590\\t\\n   591\\t\\n   592\\t(defn- parse-attr-options\\n   593\\t  [[attr & opts]]\\n   594\\t  (when-not (keyword? attr)\\n   595\\t    (throw\\n   596\\t      (ex-info\\n   597\\t        (str \\\"malformed pull pattern: attr options require keyword first, got \\\"\\n   598\\t             (pr-str attr))\\n   599\\t        {:element [attr opts]})))\\n   600\\t  (loop [opts opts\\n   601\\t         acc {:attr attr}]\\n   602\\t    (if (seq opts) (let [[k v & rest] opts] (recur rest (assoc acc k v))) acc)))\\n   603\\t\\n   604\\t\\n   605\\t(defn parse-pattern\\n   606\\t  \\\"Parse a pull pattern into a normalized spec:\\n   607\\t    {:attrs [...] :wildcard? bool :nested {...}}\\\"\\n   608\\t  [pattern]\\n   609\\t  (loop [elems pattern\\n   610\\t         acc {:attrs [], :wildcard? false, :nested {}}]\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '244,260p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md | sed -n '244,260p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   244\\t\\n   245\\tThe `:indexes` after batch *n* are a pure function of (index state before,\\n   246\\tbatch), on every host.\\n   247\\t\\n   248\\t### 2.3 What the two views mean over each medium\\n   249\\t\\n   250\\t`dao.space.query/current` and `history` are unchanged: they are\\n   251\\tinterpreters over rows, and the index state's trees are a row source.\\n   252\\t\\n   253\\t| Medium | `t` in rows | `current` | `history` |\\n   254\\t|---|---|---|---|\\n   255\\t| transactor-written (records) | allocated by the writer, monotonic | greatest-`t`-wins per `[e a v]`, retractions removed — full Datomic semantics | the exact rows; `history` promises no ordering — over a `db-value` the rows come in EAVT-walk order, not writer order — so order by `t` explicitly when it matters |\\n   256\\t| a medium whose rows carry no writer-allocated `t` (all rows `t 0`, `m default-op`) | not meaningful | the *set* of facts asserted — correct for a medium of assertions with no retractions | degenerate: all rows share `t 0`; order is the observer's batch ordinal, available through resolution facts (§3), **not** through `t` |\\n   257\\t\\n   258\\tThe observer does not paper over the second row by minting `t` — decision\\n   259\\t4. A composition that wants transaction-time semantics over such a medium\\n   260\\troutes it through a transactor (the writer allocates `t`); one that wants\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '65,125p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '65,125p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    65\\t        (keyword? x) 4\\n    66\\t        (symbol? x) 5\\n    67\\t        :else 6))\\n    68\\t\\n    69\\t\\n    70\\t(defn- compare-numbers\\n    71\\t  \\\"Portable numeric order (dao.jing.cbor/num-compare: exact, never rounded\\n    72\\t   through double; -infinity < finite < +infinity < NaN), with host compare\\n    73\\t   kept only where it answers identically: two host integers, and on the\\n    74\\t   JVM and ClojureScript two non-NaN doubles (both compare exactly, with\\n    75\\t   0.0 and -0.0 equal). Dart's double compareTo orders -0.0 below 0.0, so\\n    76\\t   Dart takes the fast path for ints only.\\\"\\n    77\\t  [a b]\\n    78\\t  (if #?(:cljd (and (dart/is? a int) (dart/is? b int))\\n    79\\t         :clj (or (and (instance? Long a) (instance? Long b))\\n    80\\t                  (and (instance? Double a) (instance? Double b)\\n    81\\t                       (not (Double/isNaN ^Double a)) (not (Double/isNaN ^Double b))))\\n    82\\t         :cljs (and (number? a) (number? b)\\n    83\\t                    (not (js/isNaN a)) (not (js/isNaN b))))\\n    84\\t    (compare a b)\\n    85\\t    (cbor/num-compare a b)))\\n    86\\t\\n    87\\t\\n    88\\t(defn compare-vals\\n    89\\t  \\\"Compare two datom values across heterogeneous types using type-rank.\\n    90\\t   Numbers compare by exact value across every kind and representation,\\n    91\\t   with no string fallback (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, Numeric\\n    92\\t   identity); ties across kinds stay ties, so (compare-vals 1 1.0) is 0.\\\"\\n    93\\t  [a b]\\n    94\\t  (let [ra (type-rank a)\\n    95\\t        rb (type-rank b)]\\n    96\\t    (cond\\n    97\\t      (not= ra rb) (compare ra rb)\\n    98\\t      (= 2 ra) (compare-numbers a b)\\n    99\\t      :else\\n   100\\t      (try (compare a b)\\n   101\\t           ;; :cljd FIRST in every reader-conditional: the cljd host-eval\\n   102\\t           ;; pass also matches :clj, so a :clj branch appearing earlier\\n   103\\t           ;; in the clause list wins on cljd too — here that would mean\\n   104\\t           ;; referencing the JVM-only ClassCastException, which doesn't\\n   105\\t           ;; exist in Dart.\\n   106\\t           (catch #?(:cljd Object\\n   107\\t                     :clj ClassCastException\\n   108\\t                     :cljs js/Error)\\n   109\\t                  _\\n   110\\t             (compare (str a) (str b)))))))\\n   111\\t\\n   112\\t\\n   113\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   114\\t;; Datom slots and sort orders\\n   115\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   116\\t\\n   117\\t(defn datom-e\\n   118\\t  [d]\\n   119\\t  (nth d 0))\\n   120\\t\\n   121\\t\\n   122\\t(defn datom-a\\n   123\\t  [d]\\n   124\\t  (nth d 1))\\n   125\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \\\"defn eavt-cmp|defn.*compare|canonical.*compare|compare-values\\\" src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_16\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \\\"defn eavt-cmp|defn.*compare|canonical.*compare|compare-values\\\" src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc src/cljc/dao/data/btree.cljc'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:70:(defn- compare-numbers\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:88:(defn compare-vals\\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:156:(defn eavt-cmp\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_17\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The current query sorts facts in EAVT order, so “first proof” means the first proof value under the datastore comparator, not the first one committed. I’m checking what happens when an entity has multiple proofs and whether that choice stays stable across hosts.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '330,350p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.schema.md | sed -n '282,297p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_18\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.query.md | sed -n '330,350p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.space.schema.md | sed -n '282,297p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   330\\t## Current-state resolution\\n   331\\t\\n   332\\tThe log is append-only, so a retraction is another d5 datom. Interpretation\\n   333\\tis explicit:\\n   334\\t\\n   335\\t- `(current source)` bounds by `as-of`, resolves the greatest `(t,m)` for\\n   336\\t  each local `[e a v]`, removes retractions, and projects to d3.\\n   337\\t- `(history source)` exposes the exact d5 relation, optionally bounded by\\n   338\\t  `as-of`.\\n   339\\t- A bare vector relation has no temporal semantics, even when every row has\\n   340\\t  five positions.\\n   341\\t\\n   342\\t`as-of` is accepted only with an explicit datom view. There is no implicit\\n   343\\tcardinality-one schema or automatic supersession.\\n   344\\t\\n   345\\t## Datalog surface\\n   346\\t\\n   347\\t`q` implements Datalog over one or more immutable relations:\\n   348\\t\\n   349\\t- **Positive-conjunction pattern clauses** — arbitrary mixed-dimensional\\n   350\\t  tuples. A plain clause matches exact arity. `[fixed ... & _]` explicitly\\n   282\\t  emits no fact and creates nothing.\\n   283\\t- **Card-many collection expansion.** A collection value on a card-many\\n   284\\t  attribute (`{:person/friends #{8 9}}`) expands to one datom per element —\\n   285\\t  the `yin.vm` precedent (`datoms->tx-data`).\\n   286\\t- **No dedup.** The wrapper does not detect no-ops: retracting an absent\\n   287\\t  fact, or re-asserting the live value, appends as told. The log is history;\\n   288\\t  interpretation decides visibility.\\n   289\\t\\n   290\\tAn attribute named by the schema but with no `:db/cardinality` declaration is\\n   291\\tcard-many: schema rows are additive constraints, undeclared means\\n   292\\tpass-through.\\n   293\\t\\n   294\\t**Strictness is a mode, not a property of the space.** Generativity is the\\n   295\\ttuple space's identity — tuples can be appended with no predefined schema —\\n   296\\tso the wrapper rejects only under an explicit strict interpretation:\\n   297\\t`(schema/transactor local-stream intake-pool {:strict true})`. The default\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '150,187p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '108,135p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc | sed -n '150,187p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc | sed -n '108,135p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   150\\t        :else (compare-vals a b)))\\n   151\\t\\n   152\\t\\n   153\\t;; Covered indexes order canonical local d5 datoms only. Stream scope belongs\\n   154\\t;; to the interpreter selecting a source, not to an appended tuple slot.\\n   155\\t\\n   156\\t(defn eavt-cmp\\n   157\\t  [d1 d2]\\n   158\\t  (let [c (cmp-field (datom-e d1) (datom-e d2))]\\n   159\\t    (if (zero? c)\\n   160\\t      (let [c (cmp-field (datom-a d1) (datom-a d2))]\\n   161\\t        (if (zero? c)\\n   162\\t          (let [c (cmp-field (datom-v d1) (datom-v d2))]\\n   163\\t            (if (zero? c)\\n   164\\t              (let [c (cmp-field (datom-t d1) (datom-t d2))]\\n   165\\t                (if (zero? c) (cmp-field (datom-m d1) (datom-m d2)) c))\\n   166\\t              c))\\n   167\\t          c))\\n   168\\t      c)))\\n   169\\t\\n   170\\t\\n   171\\t(defn aevt-cmp\\n   172\\t  [d1 d2]\\n   173\\t  (let [c (cmp-field (datom-a d1) (datom-a d2))]\\n   174\\t    (if (zero? c)\\n   175\\t      (let [c (cmp-field (datom-e d1) (datom-e d2))]\\n   176\\t        (if (zero? c)\\n   177\\t          (let [c (cmp-field (datom-v d1) (datom-v d2))]\\n   178\\t            (if (zero? c)\\n   179\\t              (let [c (cmp-field (datom-t d1) (datom-t d2))]\\n   180\\t                (if (zero? c) (cmp-field (datom-m d1) (datom-m d2)) c))\\n   181\\t              c))\\n   182\\t          c))\\n   183\\t      c)))\\n   184\\t\\n   185\\t\\n   186\\t(defn avet-cmp\\n   187\\t  [d1 d2]\\n   108\\t       (sort index/eavt-cmp)))\\n   109\\t\\n   110\\t\\n   111\\t(defn- d5->current-facts\\n   112\\t  [elements as-of]\\n   113\\t  (->> (bound-datoms (flatten-datoms elements) as-of)\\n   114\\t       current-state-seq\\n   115\\t       (mapv #(subvec (vec %) 0 3))))\\n   116\\t\\n   117\\t\\n   118\\t(defn- d5->history-rows\\n   119\\t  [elements as-of]\\n   120\\t  (mapv vec (bound-datoms (flatten-datoms elements) as-of)))\\n   121\\t\\n   122\\t\\n   123\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   124\\t;; Entity-map relation normalization (explicit d3 projection)\\n   125\\t;; =============================================================================\\n   126\\t\\n   127\\t(defn- entity-map->d3\\n   128\\t  [m]\\n   129\\t  (when-not (map? m)\\n   130\\t    (throw (ex-info \\\"entity-map-relation requires entity maps\\\" {:entity m})))\\n   131\\t  (when-not (contains? m :db/id)\\n   132\\t    (throw (ex-info \\\"raw entity-map source requires an explicit :db/id\\\"\\n   133\\t                    {:entity m})))\\n   134\\t  (let [e (:db/id m)]\\n   135\\t    (into [] (keep (fn [[a v]] (when (not= a :db/id) [e a v]))) m)))\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \\\"yin.module/envelope|yin.module/proof|events-from-datoms|authority\\\" docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md docs/design/datom.world.md | head -90'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_20\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \\\"yin.module/envelope|yin.module/proof|events-from-datoms|authority\\\" docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md docs/design/datom.world.md | head -90'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"docs/design/datom.world.md:139:consumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:20:4. **Ownership Arbitration:** Emitting a continuation does not transfer ownership. Exclusion requires a **grounded authority** — a resource the grantor's boundary actually possesses, named in the value, independent of carriers — plus an **exporting state** that fences the source before publication, and **effect fencing** with authority epochs and stable operation ids, because absence of a lapse record is never evidence of tenure.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:107: :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}     ; §7.7.3  the authority medium, named in the value\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1033:`:yin.k/custody` and reads lease facts for authority. Both `:yin.k/custody`\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1034:facts are **evidence, not authority**: an offer creates no claim, and a\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1045:### 7.7.3 The authority: what the grantor possesses\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1073:  variant, and every copy names the same one authority. The same content on\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1074:  two media does not create a second arbiter, because the authority is named\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1087:cannot wire a `dao.space` transactor as authority cannot offer\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1205:ledger is authority.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1208:the authority (epoch unreadable) must **suspend protected effect\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1216:reclaims and re-grants (`dao.lease.md`, *Restart*); **permanent** authority\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1286:   authority, §7.7.3), propose, then observe the grant, else\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1583:  transactable authority the composition offers `:yin.k/fork` only.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"## Findings\\n\\n1. **P1 — A second proof can silently suppress a valid assertion.** [authority.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc:80) keeps the first proof for each entity and never examines the rest. `dao.space` permits multiple current values for an attribute without a cardinality constraint ([query spec](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/docs/design/dao.space.query.md:330)). If an untrusted writer adds a proof value to the same entity, the selected proof can fail authentication while a valid proof remains present. That silently changes name resolution, ambiguity, and the principal’s honored sequence. Reject or explicitly diagnose multiple distinct proofs on an event entity before folding it; add a test with both a valid and an invalid proof.\\n\\n2. **P1 — Proof selection can differ across hosts.** The “first” choice also depends on the order of the handed datoms ([authority.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc:67)). The test’s `current` read orders by EAVT, including the proof *value* ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:156)); comparison of noncomparable values falls back to their string representation ([index.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:88)). With two proof maps, the chosen proof can therefore depend on host representation. The single proof tests ([ingestion test](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/test/yin/vm/linker_authority_ingestion_test.cljc:138)) do not exercise this case. Resolving the multiplicity in finding 1 without choosing a first proof removes this dependency.\\n\\n**Orphan proofs:** Ignoring a proof that has no envelope is safe for the *current* snapshot: it creates no authority event. It becomes unsafe if an envelope later appears on that entity, because the previously ignored proof joins it and participates in the first-proof choice. The new spec sentence ([linker spec](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1717)) accurately describes the code, but it codifies that unresolved behavior. The malformed-datoms test ([ingestion test](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-m4-a3/test/yin/vm/linker_authority_ingestion_test.cljc:229)) checks only an orphan on a separate entity.\\n\\nThe single-proof path follows section 8.2’s authentication and per-principal passes, and the snapshot tests exercise rebuilding from a bounded read. The reported JVM, Node, Dart, and lint results are orchestrator evidence; I did not rerun them.\\n\\n**Verdict: APPROVE-WITH-FIXES. Must fix:** reject or diagnose multiple distinct proofs per event entity, remove order-dependent proof selection, and test the case where an orphan proof is later joined by an envelope.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"turn.completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":207738,\"cached_input_tokens\":162176,\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":2521,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":364}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790431500000-vm-engineer-linker-m4-a3-fix1.claude-sonnet-5.report.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790431500000-vm-engineer-linker-m4-a3-fix1.claude-sonnet-5.report.md'","aggregated_output":"# Report: M4 A3 fix1 (claude-sonnet-5)\n\nWorktree datomworld-m4-a3, uncommitted.\n\n## Changed\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/authority.cljc` `events-from-datoms`: proofs\n  are collected per entity into a set of distinct values; an entity\n  gets a proof only if the set has exactly one value. Two or more\n  distinct values means no proof is attached, so the event is the\n  existing :no-proof case (:unauthenticated diagnostic through the\n  policy). Fail closed, no picking, no order dependence. Docstring\n  updated.\n- `docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md` (~line 1717): the \"first proof datom\"\n  sentence now states one proof value per entity, two distinct values\n  = no-proof, and an orphan proof joins an envelope that later appears\n  on its entity.\n- Test namespace `yin.vm.linker-authority-ingestion-test`, new tests:\n  - `two-distinct-proofs-on-one-entity-fail-closed` (valid + forged\n    on one entity via dao.space: one event, no proof, :no-proof discard)\n  - `ingestion-is-invariant-under-datom-permutation` (compares event\n    frequencies over reversed, rotated and shuffled inputs)\n  - `orphan-proof-joins-a-later-envelope-deterministically`\n\n## Decisions\n- Reused the :no-proof diagnostic rather than adding a new kind, since\n  section 8.2's diagnostic kinds are a closed set.\n- Event order still follows the envelope datoms' order (unchanged);\n  the permutation test compares as a multiset, and the proof choice\n  is the order-independent part.\n- Identical duplicate proof values collapse (set), so they still\n  authenticate.\n\n## Verification\n- cljstyle check clean, kondo 0 errors/warnings.\n- JVM: yin.vm.linker-authority-ingestion-test and\n  yin.vm.linker-authority-test: 24 tests, 107 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Full three lanes not run (orchestrator).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
